    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An independent Muslim kingdom that united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> delta in 1352 and ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandua-malda">Pandua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> until 1576.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, muslin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> made it rich, and its ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satgaon">Satgaon</a> traded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li><li>Its sultans patronised Bengali literature and built distinctive brick and terracotta mosques.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1414 envoys from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> presented a giraffe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where it was hailed as a qilin, a mythical creature of good omen.</p></aside>
    </section>

