    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji.jpg" alt="Bakhtiyar Khalji" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_end_of_Buddhist_Monks,_A.D._1193_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Margaret Dovaston, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muslim rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> began with the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a> around 1204, and for more than a century the province was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a> by governors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> who often rebelled against their distant overlords. In the 1330s and 1340s, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>'s empire weakened, Bengal split into rival regimes at Lakhnauti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satgaon">Satgaon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonargaon">Sonargaon</a>, the last ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhruddin-mubarak-shah">Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah</a> and his son. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-ilyas-shah">Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah</a>, who seized power at Lakhnauti in the early 1340s, took Satgaon and then Sonargaon, and by 1352 had united the whole delta under one ruler for the first time. He styled himself <em>Shah-i Bangalah</em>, and the name Bangalah came to describe the unified country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> marched against him in 1353–1354 and besieged him in the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekdala">Ekdala</a>, but withdrew without a decisive result. A second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughluq</a> invasion against Ilyas Shah's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-shah-bengal">Sikandar Shah</a> in 1359 ended in a negotiated peace, and Delhi thereafter accepted Bengal's independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilyas-shahi-dynasty">Ilyas Shahi dynasty</a> governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandua-malda">Pandua</a>, and later the old city of Gaur again became the capital, with the delta's rivers serving as both highways and defences.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhaka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhaka.jpg" alt="Dhaka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Hatirjheel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meer Sadi, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> was among the most fertile and densely populated regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Its flood-fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields produced surpluses that were exported by sea, and the delta also yielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. Fine cotton cloth, including the muslins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> region, was traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satgaon">Satgaon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> linked Bengal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. The Moroccan traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited eastern Bengal in 1346 shortly before unification, remarked on the abundance and cheapness of its food, and the Chinese interpreter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a>, who accompanied the Ming fleets in the early fifteenth century, described its markets and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins. The sultans issued large quantities of silver <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanka">tankas</a></em> from mints across the delta, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells served for small transactions. Society was mixed: a Persian-speaking Muslim ruling class of Turkish, Afghan, Abyssinian and local origin governed a mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist-descended population, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shaikhs, often granted land, were important in settling and cultivating the forested east. Hindu landholders and officials remained essential to administration, and in the countryside Bengali, not Persian, was the language of daily life and of a growing religious literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra.jpg" alt="Battle of Ghaghra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra">Battle of Ghaghra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Ghagra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pardeepsingh2657, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultans' military strength rested on the geography of the delta. Their armies combined infantry levies, known as <em>paiks</em>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and river fleets, while cavalry horses had to be imported from the north and were always fewer than in the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a>. Rivers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> were the best defence: invaders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in the 1350s found that they could not hold the country through the rains, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-ilyas-shah">Ilyas Shah</a> withstood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a> by retreating into the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekdala">Ekdala</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> also fought its neighbours. The sultans contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a> and Arakan; in the 1420s and 1430s Bengal backed the Arakanese king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/min-saw-mon">Min Saw Mon</a> in recovering his throne, and Arakan paid tribute for a time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a> (r. 1494–1519) conquered the kingdom of Kamata in 1498 and campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, Tripura and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley, though attempts to subdue the Ahom kingdom failed. The rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> in the north brought harder enemies: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> defeated a Bengal army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra">Battle of the Ghaghra</a> in 1529, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Khan Sur</a> defeated the sultan's forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-surajgarh">Surajgarh</a> in 1534 and twice besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a>, taking it in 1538 despite Portuguese help to the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husain-shahi-dynasty">Husain Shahi</a> sultan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jalaluddin-muhammad-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jalaluddin-muhammad-shah.jpg" alt="Jalaluddin Muhammad Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jalaluddin-muhammad-shah">Jalaluddin Muhammad Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_Coin_of_Jalaluddin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rani nurmai, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultans ruled as independent Muslim monarchs, reading the Friday sermon and striking coins in their own names, and some styled themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> on their coinage. Persian was the language of government. The kingdom was divided into provinces and districts under military governors and revenue officials, many holding assignments of revenue in return for service. Several dynasties succeeded one another. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilyas-shahi-dynasty">Ilyas Shahis</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> landholder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ganesha">Raja Ganesha</a> took power around 1414; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalaluddin-muhammad-shah">Jadu</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and ruled as Jalaluddin Muhammad Shah until 1433. The Ilyas Shahi line was restored in 1435, but Abyssinian palace guards, introduced in large numbers in the later fifteenth century, seized the throne in 1487, and several Habshi rulers were murdered in quick succession. Order returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a>, an official said to be of Arab descent, chosen by the nobles in 1494, whose dynasty ruled until 1538. Husain Shah employed Hindus in high office, among them the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupa-goswami">Rupa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanatana-goswami">Sanatana</a>, who later became leading theologians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnava</a> movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya</a>. The sultans also corresponded with foreign courts, exchanging embassies with the Ming emperors and, according to a well-known tradition, inviting the Persian poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/adina-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adina-mosque.jpg" alt="Adina Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/adina-mosque">Adina Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adina_Mosque_at_Malda_district_of_West_Bengal_08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amitabha Gupta, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate's greatest legacy was the political and cultural unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> as a single country with its own identity, distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a>. Its court patronised literature in Bengali as well as Persian: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maladhar-basu">Maladhar Basu</a> composed the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-krishna-vijaya">Sri Krishna Vijaya</a></em> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukunuddin-barbak-shah">Rukunuddin Barbak Shah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krittibas-ojha">Krittibas Ojha</a> produced the most popular Bengali <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a></em> in this era, and the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Husain Shah</a> sponsored a Bengali rendering of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a></em>. Muslim poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-muhammad-sagir">Shah Muhammad Sagir</a> adapted Persian romances into Bengali. The sultans also created a distinctive architecture of brick and terracotta, with curved cornices and many domes, seen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adina-mosque">Adina Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandua-malda">Pandua</a>, completed in the 1370s and among the largest mosques of the subcontinent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixty-dome-mosque">Sixty Dome Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagerhat">Bagerhat</a>, associated with the frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> Khan Jahan Ali, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhoto-sona-mosque">Chhoto Sona Mosque</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakhil-darwaza">Dakhil Darwaza</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a>. Bengal's diplomacy reached far: in 1414 a Bengali embassy presented a giraffe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The sultanate also laid foundations for the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> of eastern Bengal, which historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-eaton">Richard Eaton</a> connect with land settlement on the forest frontier, a process that accelerated under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chausa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chausa.jpg" alt="Battle of Chausa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chausa">Battle of Chausa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_plan_of_Chausa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Beylarbey, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independent sultanate ended in the struggle for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> that followed the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah">Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah</a> (r. 1519–1533) lost ground to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyasuddin-mahmud-shah">Ghiyasuddin Mahmud Shah</a> faced the Afghan leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Khan Sur</a>, who defeated his army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-surajgarh">Surajgarh</a> in 1534 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> in 1538. The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> occupied Gaur briefly before Sher Shah defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chausa">Chausa</a> in 1539, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> became a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">the Sur Empire</a>. When Sur power broke up in the mid-1550s, Afghan governors in Bengal declared independence and revived the sultanate under new dynasties. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karrani-dynasty">Karrani Afghans</a>, who took Gaur in 1564 and were led from about 1566 by Sulaiman Khan Karrani, ruled from Gaur and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanda-bengal">Tanda</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> in 1568 and outwardly acknowledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> to avoid war. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a> defied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tukaroi">Tukaroi</a> in 1575 and was captured and executed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rajmahal">Battle of Rajmahal</a> in July 1576, ending the sultanate. Mughal control remained incomplete for decades, as the Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a>, a confederacy of eastern landholders led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a>, resisted in the delta into the early seventeenth century. Bengal then became one of the richest provinces of the Mughal Empire, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> from 1610.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Bengal Sultanate matters because it first united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> delta as one independent state, Bangalah, and for over two centuries made it a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, textile and maritime power whose court patronised Bengali literature, built distinctive brick mosques and shaped the Muslim society of eastern Bengal.</em></p>

